A||||51||batboy||more computer overclocking||||6||
Z||000000||batboy||02-02-2001||08:19 PM||||Ok, I'll admit it, I'm becoming a computer hardware and overclocking geek. Since this is really sort of computer hotrodding, I suppose it's natural that I'd have to hotrod something in the winter.<P>I know Cliff is overclocking a Celeron 566 (like the chip I have) to at least 850. What are some of you other guys reaching? What did you have to do to get there?<P><BR>I've been getting extreme lately. I've wired pins on the CPU to increase the default voltage. My computer sounds like a old airplane preparing for take-off, because I have so many fans going (7 total). I changed over from a Golden Orb to a massive GlobalWin FOP38 cooler with Arctic Silver thermal paste, I've shortened and rounded my flat cables and rerouted all my power wires for better air flow. I even have a fan and heatsink on the motherboard and on the GeForce II video card. This pic shows 5 of the 7 fans. That black thing next to the cards is a slot fan.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/comp-innards.jpg"> <P><BR>   <BR>Playing Unreal for 15 minutes, my CPU temp used to run 40 degrees C. and the motherboard temp was 35. Now, the CPU is a mere 32.5 and the mobo is 24 after 15 minutes of Unreal (case off for both sets of measurements with ambient temp of 20 degrees C).<P>Well, after torturing this C-566 CPU chip, and trying every overclocking trick and tweak I could learn about, plus running the CPU core at 1.9v (default is 1.5v)...I finally hit 1020 MHz or 1.02 gig! For the first time ever, I got it to boot into Windows. With trembling hands, I moved the mouse...it locked up...LOL! Not exactly stable. But it is stable enough at 978 MHz that I can run benchmarks and games.<P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/C-566at1020.jpg">   <BR><p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-02-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||02-02-2001||09:16 PM||||Did you sand your processor? That helps cooling it <B>a lot</B>.||||reg||14
Z||000002||Greg Piet||02-02-2001||09:33 PM||||I was going to overclock my 550 Athlon Slot A, then realized I would likely only get to 750-850, and I can get an 850 processor pretty cheap.<P>But Kudos on the overclocking!||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||02-02-2001||09:39 PM||||Cliff, yes I lapped or sanded the top of the chip core that touches the heatsink...a little bit. I've heard of people getting too carried away and ruining chips this way, so I wanted to be careful.<P><BR>Under magnification, the center of the chip was slightly raised and the rim was a little higher than the rest. I used some 1000 grade sandpaper and then 1200 and knocked down the high spots a bit. On the other hand, I went all out on lapping the heatsink. It's now mirror smooth.<P><BR>I have two different CPU cooler fans that I use depending on the situation. My extreme overclocking fan is a Delta 60mm that moves 38 cfm of air, but jeez, is it loud (46 db). For normal 952 MHz usage, I run a Sunon 26 cfm 60mm fan that is much quieter.<P>I want a stable 1+ gig computer, so I'm going to buy two of the new cCO stepping Celerons that are supposed to be much better overclockers than the earlier Celeron II cBO chips like Cliff and I have.<P>I'm getting one 600 and one 633, then cross my fingers and hope I get lucky with one of them. I have a buddy who will buy the other one if it'll at least make 100 bus speed, which is pretty much a sure thing with these Celly's.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||wkayl||02-02-2001||09:59 PM||||I know how to boot up my computer.  The most I get into is replacing components like modems, hard drives and so forth.  At this time I have a PentIII 733mhz with a 40 gig 7200rpm h/d 128 megs of ram.  Good enough for me at this time.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||02-02-2001||11:58 PM||||I know that some of you (BB, Cliff) are geniuses (genii?) when it comes to overclocking (at least you impress me with all of that digibabble <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), but I just bought a new Gateway with a 1.3Gig P4 (with all the toys) for less than $1600 shipped to my door.  Whisper quiet, too.  Is there any other reason you guys overclock computers other than to say that you did it?  Not trying to be an ass, just curious.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||02-03-2001||01:14 AM||||MM, I'd be curious to hear what video and sound cards, etc. that machine came with. I just read an article over at Tom's Hardware about Gateway, Dell, and other OEM computer builders slapping cheap inferior parts into these budget P-4's. Download Sandra (a benchmarking program) or go to the Wintune website. I'd be interested in knowing what your 3D video benchmarks are.<P>Gamers like running a higher than spec bus so the PCI cards and the AGP vid card runs faster too. Then we put in a GeForce II 3D card and overclock that too. Right now, a good BX or 815 motherboard is faster than a P-4 board. Overclocked P-III's in the 1.1 gig range usually can whip up on the P-4's in most benchmarks.<P>Besides, I like the idea of being able to buy a $75 Celeron II 600 CPU and having a chance to overclock it to 1008 MHz. I think the P-III 1 gig chips are selling for 5-6 times that much.<P>My buddy at work decided to out overclock me and bought a new fancy motherboard and an AMD Thunderbird 900 that he's running at 1008. I can run my C-566 at 978 fairly stable, so we compared benchmarks. I totally creamed him in the video scores. Especially the 3D stuff, blew him away. It's kind of like horsepower vs. torque.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||02-03-2001||01:28 AM||||Here's the link for that review I was talking about. Tom's Hardware is one of the older and more respected computer hardware review sites.<P><BR> <A HREF="http://www.tomshardware.com/cpu/01q1/010130/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tomshardware.com/cpu/01q1/010130/index.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||LarryB||02-03-2001||03:25 AM||||I read the same article early this afternoon that batboy is alluding to.  The $1600 Athlon box held its own against the $2600 P4 box, never mind the $1600 P4 machine. <P>The machine I'm using right now to post this is a 1.2GHz Athlon @ 1300 MHz. I'm using a cooler from <A HREF="http://www.3dfxcool.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.3dfxcool.com</A>  - I noticed batboy has a fan from them in his case - and it works great.  This box is a real screamer. I also have a Celeron 566@952MHz that uses a GlobalWin FOP-32 to keep that processor cool.  Not quite as good as the FOP-38 but not as loud either (the difference between the 2 is the fan. The heatsinks are identical.)  That processor posts at 1063 MHz but Windows 98 crashes during bootup, this is on an Abit BX6 board that has 100/103/112/124/133 MHz FSB settings. For what I want out of this machine 952MHz is fantastic, and its proven to be completely reliable. <P>So far I've found Arctic Silver not to be worth the expense, haven't found any measurable difference yet in how it works vs a good quality thermal paste properly applied.<P>Why do I overclock? Because I can.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  There are some situations where the potential tradeoffs are worth it. <P>Oh, BTW.  That fan that batboy has mounted in the PCI slot that exhausts out is pretty widely regarded as a piece of junk, my own experience follows that trend.  You'll be lucky if it doesn't burn out and sieze within a year. ||||reg||10
Z||000009||batboy||02-03-2001||03:51 AM||||Hey Larry, I know that slot fan ain't worth a lot, but it does a fair job of exhausting hot air from my overclocked video card. You're right about the Arctic Silver being a bit overrated. I went from that crappy thermal tape to a good silicone thermal paste and that made a big difference. Then I changed over to Arctic Silver and only got lower CPU temp of 1 degree (both at idle and under load). Not as much as I had been lead to believe, however, it did make it run a little bit cooler and that is a good thing when you're pushing the extreme.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||02-03-2001||04:20 AM||||I've got my k6-2 300 running at 350  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> not much but it's the best she'll do.. heh.  I'm probably gonna be throwing a  500mhz chip in here pretty soon, since the mobo supports it and they're only like $36.. or i'll go to a k6-3 500.  Eventually I'm gonna build up a thunderbird 800 or 900, since AMD has been kicking Intel's butt all over the place lately..<P>Batboy, what do you run on 3dMark?  I have a friend with an Athlon 650 and a Geforce 2mx who's just a bit under 5800.<P>As a side note, anyone hear about Moore's law being thrown out the window?  Technology is improving <B>fast</B>!  For those that don't know.. Moore's Law states that the processing power of a computer chip will double every 18 months.||||reg||
Z||000011||Cliff Pennock||02-03-2001||07:51 AM||||Monkeyman, to a certain point you are not <B>over</B>clocking your computer, you are just removing the <B>under</B>clocking by Intel.<P><B>All</B> processor chips in a certain speed range are actually the <B>exact</B> same processors. For instance, there's no physical difference between a Celeron 333Mhz and a Celeron 500Mhz, they are identical. The only reason one runs at lower speed is because Intel has locked it at a lower speed.<P>Let me try to explain in short how processors work.<P>A processor has its own set of <I>operations</I>. An operation is something like: <I>add two numbers</I>, <I>move a byte from one location to another</I>, <I>read the level of a pin</I>, etc. A combination of operations is called an <I>instruction</I>. For instance, <I>read the level of a pin</I> <B>and</B> <I>store it in a location</I> is a single instruction, but two operations. Operations take time to complete so before you can start the next operation, you need to wait until the previous operation has finished. The time you wait should be long enough to allow the longest operation to finish. This time-span is called a <B>clock-cycle</B>. The start of a cycle is signaled by an electronic pulse, the <B>clock-pulse</B>. The speed with which two clock-pulses follow each other is the <B>clock-speed</B>.<P>So the highest theoretical clock-speed a processor can run at, is when the time between two clock-pulses is the same time it takes for the longest operation to finish.<P>So a processor running at a 300Mhz clock-speed is getting 300,000,000 clock-pulses per second. This means that the longest operation should be finished within 1/300,000,000th of a second.<P>Due to design history, a processor only gets a part of these clock-pulses from the motherboard. The motherboard either gives 66 million, 100 million or 133 million (and some other values) pulses per second to the processor. This is called the <B>bus-speed</B>. The processor itself has some electronics that measures the time between two external pulses and add extra pulses itself. For instance, to double the number of clock-pulses, it measures the time between two external pulses, divides that time by two, and generates its own extra pulses when that time has expired. The number it uses to divide the time of two external pulses, is called the <B>clock-multiplier</B> (because it effectively multiplies the clock-speed). So for instance a Celeron 300 has a bus-speed of 66Mhz and a multiplier of 4.5.<P>Now back to the manufacturing process.<P>When Intel designs a chip it has a theoretical maximum clock-speed. This maximum is derived from the maximum time the longest operation takes to finish. Physically an operation is nothing more than throwing a lot of mini, mini switches or <B>changing transistors-states</B>. Some operations requires changing the states of a lot of transistors, other operations require only few transistors. So the time an operation takes is depending on the <I>number</I> of transistors needed for an operation and the <I>size</I> of the transistors (compare it with an ordinary door; a larger door takes longer to open/close than a smaller door).<P>Transistors on a processor are very, very small, so small the size is measured in molecules. If a transistor is a few molecules larger/thicker than intended, it can have a significant impact on the time it takes to change states. So a processor that has all perfect transistors can run at its highest theoretical speed. If a processor has a lot of imperfect transistors, it can only run at lower speeds.<P>Processors are manufactured on <I>wafers</I>, round CD like printed circuit boards. Processors near the edge of the wafer were almost always imperfect and were automatically designated lower speeds (even though they were the exact same processors as in the middle of the wafer). But production processes have improved a lot and nowadays the processors at the edge of the wafer are almost as good as those in the middle. But still the processors at the edge are automatically designated lower speeds than those in the middle because of the small chance it could be imperfect. Because they were given lower speed-ratings, the price of these processors were much lower than the processors in the middle of the wafer.<P>But then the word got out. People started realizing the "slower" processors where the same as the "faster" processor and would probably run perfectly on those faster speeds. People where starting to "overclock" the hell out of their processors - or actually running it at their design speed. Every now and then it would not run at their design speed but that was just tough luck. Intel of course didn't like this because they weren't selling any of their more expensive "faster" processors. So they thought of a way to "lock" processors to a certain speed. They had no control over the bus-speed, since this was controlled by the motherboard, but they did have control over the multiplier since this was an on-chip deal. So an extra step was added to the manufacturing process. After the wafer was made, they set like a mini-jumper on the processor, locking the multiplier. This made overclocking a lot more difficult since it wasn't so easy for users to tamper with the bus-speed. The bus-speed controls everything on your motherboard so increasing your bus-speed would actually overclock everything in your computer. Motherboard manufacturers saw an opportunity to increase sales amongst overclockers: make sure the bus-speed for your processor is independent of the bus-speed for the rest of your computer. Abit was the first to do that and it's still a very popular brand amongst overclockers.<P>Because Intel keeps improving it's manufacturing process, chances are very good that you can run <B>any</B> processor at it's designed speed. Yes, I have a Celeron 566 running at 850. It's not overclocked, it's running at it's design speed. Everything above 850Mhz is actually overclocking because you are then running your processor outside it's specification. The maximum you can overclock a processor to is it's theoretical sped, anything above that is breaking the laws of nature. It could very well be that the theoretical maximum speed of a Celeron 566 is somewhere near a few Ghz. But this means all-perfect transistors and super-cooling (the speed of free-flowing electrons increases with lower temperatures of the molecules they hop between).<P>So to make a very long story short, the reason I overclock is.. well... I don't overclock. I'm running my processor at the speed it is designed to run at, at a much lower price than Intel wants me to pay for that speed.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phil||02-03-2001||09:03 AM||||Oh||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||02-03-2001||11:17 AM||||Yeah, what Cliff said...<P>Also, the Celeron line is the same CPU chip as the P-III, except half the L-2 cache has been castrated. Yep, a Celeron 600 is the same exact chip with the same 9X clock multiplier as the P-III 900. Run the C-600 at 100 MHz bus speed instead of the "default" 66 MHz and suddenly you have a 900 MHz processor (9X66=600 and 9X100=900).<P>Since front side bus speeds at 66, 100, and 133 are default settings for various OEM processors, if you run one of these bus speeds, that means your PCI and AGP bus are running with recommended specs too. The computer components are happy, you are happy, but Intel's pissed.<P>Right now, a P-4 1.4 gig CPU is selling retail for over a grand. If you could buy another chip at a small fraction of that cost and when overclocked it nearly matches or might even beat the P-4 performance, then why spend more?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||02-03-2001||11:51 AM||||Mine is a BP6 with two CEL-I 533's, overclocked to 600. This with the normal CPU core voltage. Temps run around 30*C, with generic fans/heatsinks. Did use thermal compound, though. I can get it to run at 666, but POST will only detect one processor. I'm told that 600 was about the max design spec for these chips, so I don't feel too bad about it.<P>Wish I would have waited, though.<BR>ABIT is now shipping the VP6. Dual S370s.<BR>Board is going for $175 or less, and getting rave reviews. I understand that PIII 600s (overclockable to hell 'n' back) are going for $120. So for just over $400, you've got the makings of a killer dual CPU system. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
