Z||000021||FieroSTETZ||02-04-2001||03:29 AM||||amd k6-3/400 @1.2ghz- have a device that plugs into the zif socket and into the power supply that goes between the cpu and the mobo and allows you to change the multiplier up to 12x, and i have an aluminum water jacket w/ peltiers... runs real good for a 30 dollar cpu. also have a p3/533eb overclocked to 933- used a couple of those slot fans batboy showed, and a small fan on the top, w/ every crack sealed- creates a STRONG vacuum inside, then i just run flexible black tubing to the hot parts (cold air induction!) and its like reverse fans||||reg||1
Z||000022||Formula88||02-04-2001||11:19 AM||||I'm running a Pentium III 600e at 800 MHz.  Not very impressive overclocking, but it's a rock stable as a factory 800EB.  As a matter of fact, the BIOS *thinks* it's an 800EB!  I've gotten it up to about 900MHz, but crashes start becoming a problem.  Runing an Asus P3V4X mobo, 256 Mb Micron PC133 SDRAM, and a bunch of add in cards.  One of my cards is likely the culprit in my speed limit, but for now I'm happy with 800.<P>------------------<BR>We all come into this world the same way; naked, screaming, and covered in blood.  If you're lucky, it doesn't have to end there.||||reg||6
Z||000023||TheFiero64||02-04-2001||11:34 AM||||I have my system overclocked to 374MHz from a 333. I have been playing and trying to get more than that out of it and can't seem to do it <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. I have a AMDK6-2 333MHz. I am looking at a nice AMDK6-3 450MHz online now <P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000024||Cooter||02-04-2001||01:12 PM||||I have bumped my Xeon 400 up to 450 a time or two. Runs fine, but not much difference. Anything above 450 causes trouble- anyone with tips for this CPU, I will listen.||||reg||1
Z||000025||LarryB||02-04-2001||03:02 PM||||Depends if the problem is with your processor or with your memory.  A lot of PC-100 memory, especially older or generic memory, won't handle much more than 112MHz FSB.   (I have 1 64MB PC-100 stick that won't even handle THAT.)  If you have or can borrow a stick of PC-133 memory you can give that a shot.  I wouldn't run out and buy a stick just to try this. <P>The other common stumbling block with the P-II core (I'm pretty sure a Xeon 400 uses a P-II core) is with the external cache.  If your BIOS supports tweaking the cache latency you can try experimenting with that as well.  With the exception of the 1st gen Celerons its unusual to get a Pentium-II core over 504MHz anyways. <P>As for the comments on the AMD K-2 processors, they tend to run very hot to begin with and are generally not considered to be good overclockers.  Earlier Durons (600-650MHz) and Thunderbirds (~900MHz) often do turn out to be good overclockers. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>I have bumped my Xeon 400 up to 450 a time or two. Runs fine, but not much difference. Anything above 450 causes trouble- anyone with tips for this CPU, I will listen.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000026||batboy||02-04-2001||06:39 PM||||The main overclocking secret to reaching the next speed level if your CPU is being stubborn is too first get a better than average CPU cooler (Alpha and GlobalWin are a couple of the top brands right now). Then use a good quality thermal paste, add at least one more intake case fan, and one more exhaust case fan. Finally, once you have a cool running rig, then goose the voltage us a couple notches and watch it fly.||||reg||1
Z||000027||batboy||02-04-2001||06:46 PM||||The main overclocking secret to reaching the next speed level if your CPU is being stubborn is too first get a better than average CPU cooler (Alpha and GlobalWin are a couple of the top brands right now). Then use a good quality thermal paste, add at least one more intake case fan, and one more exhaust case fan. Finally, once you have a cool running rig, then goose the voltage us a couple notches and watch it fly.||||reg||1
Z||000028||FieroMaster88||02-05-2001||03:39 AM||||OK, i just got a new mobo today.  It will support up 750 MHz pIII (according to the book).  I was going to buy a 733 MHz processor for it, but after reading this I was wondering if getting a "slower" processor and overcloking it would be the better and cheaper way to go.  Any info would be greatly appreciated!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">   <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles<BR>84 Indy Fiero (restoration Project)||||reg||5
Z||000029||Tigger||02-05-2001||09:56 AM||||No overclocking needed here.<P>Not to brag, but I just put together an AMD 1000 Athlon system (about 2-1/2 times cheaper than a P3 1000   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> ).  It's running on one of the best motherboards for overclockers, ASUS A7V VIA Socket A.  Easy board to mess with clock settings but it's a pain to get IRQ's setup right.<P>Hmmm...  could try overclocking it to 1.21 jigahertz     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 02-05-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000030||LarryB||02-05-2001||04:24 PM||||There are no guarantees with overclocking. Anything you get above and beyond the rated performance is a roll of the dice. <P>If your motherboard supports changing the FSB speed in 1 or 2 MHz increments the risks are pretty minimal, since you can tweak the processor pretty close to its realistic potential. If the motherboard only offers 66/75/83/100/103/112/133MHz settings for example the risk is bigger. If you were thinking about getting a 733MHz CPU I personally would probably get a 700MHz P-III, a good fan (not a golden orb!) and some PC-133 memory.  The odds are good you'd get to at at least 784MHz, pretty good to 868, and based on others experiences you'd have a realistic shot to get to 933.  At the absolute worst case you'd get 721MHz with the 3% turbo setting.<P>Or you could just spend the extra $100 and get a P-III rated at 933 MHz.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Be sure to check and see if there's a newer BIOS available for your motherboard that offers official support for faster P-III processors as well.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroMaster88:<BR><B>OK, i just got a new mobo today.  It will support up 750 MHz pIII (according to the book).  I was going to buy a 733 MHz processor for it, but after reading this I was wondering if getting a "slower" processor and overcloking it would be the better and cheaper way to go.  Any info would be greatly appreciated!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000031||TheFiero64||02-05-2001||05:55 PM||||LarryB, Why not the golden orb?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||5
Z||000032||DaRkLoRD||02-05-2001||05:57 PM||||Anyone else here using liquid cooling for their setup?<P>I built a simple water cooling rig last year, but it wasn't designed for a slot CPU. I was going to buy a socket AMD to build a test box, but I ran out of money.. Now, water cooling is a bit more mainstream, and there are dozens of places selling radiators, waterblocks, etc.. it's not really "innovative" anymore.. so, the next step is...<P>total submersion in the coolant.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>(non-conductive coolant of course)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000033||LarryB||02-05-2001||06:51 PM||||A golden orb is fine as a stock cooler but its performance is run of the mill. Overclocking produces more-than-usual heat, so you need a better-than-average cooler like a GlobalWin FOP-32 or an Alpha PAL6035, for example. (Or a GlobalWin FOP-38, if you can stand the noise of the fan!)<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TheFiero64:<BR><B>LarryB, Why not the golden orb?<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000034||TheFiero64||02-05-2001||06:58 PM||||Great, I just won an auction for a golden orb. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> It was ten bucks. I just got a 450MHz processor too. Can't wait for that to come in<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000035||Cooter||02-05-2001||08:15 PM||||Having the heat generating components submerged in a cooling liquid has been around for years. The Cray "super computers" used it about 10 years ago. I believe that it used liquid freon, but it has been too long ago to remember.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Cliff Pennock||02-05-2001||09:32 PM||||Some serious cooling: <A HREF="http://cpusite.examedia.nl/sections/steve/its-cooling-time.html" TARGET=_blank>here</A>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Cliff Pennock (edited 02-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000037||DaRkLoRD||02-05-2001||10:24 PM||||Cooter, true.. but it's still FAR from mainstream.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
Z||000038||DaRkLoRD||02-05-2001||10:28 PM||||Cliff, that project was originally posted on Slashdot.org sometime in the spring of 1999.. and it's the exact article which inspired me to build my own cooler.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>everyone, that article is amazing.. if you're interested in overclocking or modifying your computer, or just into building cool stuff, READ THAT ARTICLE!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000039||batboy||02-07-2001||07:38 PM||||FieroMaster, like was already said, the P-III 700e is the hot ticket right now. Most will overclock to 900 to 950, especially if you get a retail box CPU that is what the call the cC0 stepping (default voltage is 1.7 compared to the cB0's 1.65. Also, both the 600e and the 750e are good overclockers too. Personally, I'd stay away from the ones that use 133 MHz bus speeds and stick with the 100 MHz ones.<P>The Golden Orb was the rage last spring and summer, but now there's much better coolers. For extreme O/Cing, you have to add case fans and use a good CPU cooler.||||reg||1
Z||000040||Standard||02-08-2001||01:18 AM||||Well, looks like I got my k6-2 300 stable at 400Mhz.  For some reason the core voltage was at 2.35v, dropped it down to 2.1v and it's runnin' pretty good.  Got a util to o/c my voodoo banshee card, too.  Isn't stable at anything over 120mhz core and 125mhz memory.||||reg||1
Z||000041||loafer87gt||02-08-2001||01:32 AM||||I just noticed that Intel prices were slashed again. I'd like to upgrade my old P3 500 to something approaching the 1 gig mark. The problem lies in the fact that my 512 megs of memory is Mircron PC 100 ECC. What is the fastest p3 chip out there that will run at the 100 megahertz bus speed? Are the one gig p3 chips 10x100 or 7.5x133? Right now I am running a P3 500 with an ASUS P2B motherboard.||||reg||1
Z||000042||LarryB||02-08-2001||10:42 AM||||The official datasheet says 900MHz, but I've never actually seen one. 850s are commonly available, just go to <A HREF="http://www.pricewatch.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pricewatch.com</A>  then go to <A HREF="http://www.resellerratings.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.resellerratings.com</A>  to weed out the scumbag vendors.  I see the occasional rumor about the 1 GHz processors also being available with 100MHz FSB but again I've never seen one or heard of anyone else actually saying they had one, only with 133MHz FSB.<P>BTW Intel last week announced stepping D0 and EOL'd a bunch of the slower processors, including all the P-II core Celerons and the C566.  They also revamped their prices on Monday, cutting prices mostly on their faster processors. For example the 1GHz P-III price cut was a reported 42%. <P>You can thank AMD for a lot of this pricing pressure.  The Durons are an almost unbelievable value (besides being better performers than the Celerons) and the Athlons pretty much own the P-III.  They also compete well with the P-IV at half the price (with cheaper memory and motherboards to boot!) <P>Regardless of which camp you're in, with this kind of agressive pricing we're all winners.<P>BTW, the P2B is a nice motherboard.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||10
Z||000043||TheFiero64||02-08-2001||11:02 AM||||The best cooling thing I've ever installed is Rain 1.0. Jaygee79's overclocked CPU ran so hot I could almost burn myself. I installed Rain and It is cool to the touch. I notice no slowing affect either. It is free online just do a search.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000044||batboy||02-08-2001||06:59 PM||||Hey Larry, I'd be very interested in finding out more about that new stepping. I went to the Intel site and found no press releases or any other info about it.<P>Also, according to the Intel S-spec chart, there is supposed to be a 100 MHz bus 1 gig P-III, but like the 900, I've never seen one for sell anywhere yet.||||reg||1
Z||000045||loafer87gt||02-08-2001||07:13 PM||||Well, I'm about to become an AMD user. After seeing the price difference between Intel and AMD and the apparent speed difference in 3D Apps I ordered a Thunderbird 1.2 Gig chip and Asus A7V133 motherboard. I can't wait to try it out. Boy things have sure changed over the past five years! I can remember not too long ago when my old Amiga 3000 running at 16 megahertz with 18 megs of ram was a speed demon! Anyways, I've never really paid much attention to AMD stuff, but my older bro told me you can set the FSB and the memory speed independently. So I can use my PC 100 ECC memory and still run the chip at 9.5x133 I checked out some benchmarks over at Toms hardware and my 3D rendering should be about 3x faster!!!||||reg||1
Z||000046||batboy||02-08-2001||08:15 PM||||Nothing wrong with the AMDs, they rock. I've thought about going over to the "other side" from time to time too. Last time I looked, the AMD motherboards were more expensive, which partly nullified that lower CPU prices. Also, they run hotter than the Intels, so you have to get serious about cooling solutions if you plan on overclocking them.||||reg||1
Z||000047||LarryB||02-08-2001||10:54 PM||||I caught it in a product change notification (PCN), here's the url to the pdf (Adobe Acrobat) file:<BR> <A HREF="http://developer.intel.com/design/pcn/Processors/D0001051.pdf" TARGET=_blank>http://developer.intel.com/design/pcn/Processors/D0001051.pdf</A> <P>It looks like 850MHz and 1.0GHz parts will be released to the retail channel around March 5th.<P>The AMD processors use the same higher end air coolers or slightly modified versions as used for the P-III, for example the mentioned GlobalWin FOP-38, or the FOP-32 for the P-III and the FOP-32-1 for the Thunderbird/Duron.  The primary difference seems to be slightly different clips. <P>You do need to be much more careful with the AMD processors. A high end Thunderbird can cook itself in 5-10 seconds if powered up without a heatsink attached. (for those of you who don't know what a Thunderbird is, it's the socket-A Athlon.) <BR> <BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by batboy:<BR><B>Hey Larry, I'd be very interested in finding out more about that new stepping. I went to the Intel site and found no press releases or any other info about it.<P>Also, according to the Intel S-spec chart, there is supposed to be a 100 MHz bus 1 gig P-III, but like the 900, I've never seen one for sell anywhere yet.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000048||FIEROCIOUS||02-09-2001||12:29 AM||||Um, I have a 233......nevermind.?||||reg||1
Z||000049||Tigger||02-09-2001||01:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by loafer87gt:<BR><B>Well, I'm about to become an AMD user.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Loafer I have that board too.  If you run into some confict problems where your computer freezes after you load the sound card drivers (Sound Blaster Live on mine), let me know.  I had to drop the sound card in slot 3, flashed the bios, and changed the bios to dedicate IRQ 5 for a legacy device.  Works great now, but boy was it a pain trying to figure out what was making it whig out.<P>Also, I found these sites helped me a bunch.<P><A HREF="http://www.asus.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ASUS.com</A><BR>Get latest drivers and stuff here.<P><A HREF="http://pub26.ezboard.com/bjuggernautshardware" TARGET=_blank>A7V forum</A><BR>Hang out with computer geeks here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000050||Standard||02-09-2001||03:21 AM||||I d/l'd Rain 1.0 and it really works!  Dropped the temp on my comp from 40* C to about 31* C while surfin' around.  Got it from  <A HREF="http://www.tweakfiles.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tweakfiles.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000051||TheFiero64||02-09-2001||12:09 PM||||I've always had AMD's. I just got an AMDK6-3 450 on ebay. I currently have a K6-2 333 overclocked to 375 it runs super cool. My GF has a K6-2 200 and it is at 233. It ran hot until I installed rain.||||reg||10
